ou ever thought about what she might be thinking about you? Why would she go to your house to talk to you when she can see you every day at the office?\" [2456] \"I don't know. She couldn't possibly think I'm involved in this murder case. She wouldn't.\" [2457] \"Did she accidentally hear something and make a wrong judgment?\" [2458] \"No! I tell you, no!\" [2459] I was skeptical. I couldn't help but doubt... even at this moment, I didn't believe that Hillary was telling the truth. [2460] \"Do you have enemies? Young people who are angry with you, jealous girls, or someone who is psychologically unbalanced and wants to take revenge on you?\" [2461] It's hard to believe I could say these words. [2462] \"Of course not.\" [2463] That's the way it is. Even now, I'm not entirely sure about that clock. It's a strange story. Four thirteen. What does that mean? Why include that number and those two words: remember on the postcard? Unless it had some meaning to the person who sent the postcard? [2464] I sighed, settled the bill, and stood up. [2465] \"Don't worry,\" I said (which is one of the least sincere things one can say in English or any other language), \"Colin Lamb Private Services will continue to work. You'll be fine, we'll get married, and live happily ever after, just a year from now. By the way, \" I said, unable to stop myself, even though I knew a more romantic ending would be better, but Colin Lamb's insatiable curiosity drove me to do so. \"What did you do with that clock? Did you hide it in your sock drawer?\" [2466] She hesitated for a moment, then said,\n[2467] \"I threw it in the trash bin of the neighbor's house next door.\" [2468] I was somewhat surprised. That was neat and clean. It shows that she's smart. Maybe, I underestimated Hilary. [2469] Chapter Twenty-Four\n[2470] Colin Blue's Narrative\n[2471] 1\n[2472] After Shira left, I went straight back to the Clarendon, packed my bags, and handed them to the staff. This hotel is particularly attentive to whether you will check out before noon. [2473] Then I set off. As I passed by the police station, I hesitated for a moment and decided to go in. I found Hakast there, who was frowning and looking at a letter in his hand. [2474] \"I'm leaving again tonight, Dick,\" I said. \"Back to London.\" [2475] He looked up at me thoughtfully. [2476]\"Do you want to hear some advice?\" he asked. [2477]\"No,\" I replied immediately. [2478]He didn't pay attention to me. People who want to give advice usually act this way. [2479]\"You should leave and stay away if you know what's best for you.\" [2480]\"No one can judge what's best for others.\" [2481] \"I'll keep my opinion.\" [2482] \"I want to tell you something, Dick. After completing the current task, I plan to resign. At least, I hope to resign.\" [2483] \"Why?\" [2484] \"I feel like a Victorian-era old priest. I'm very suspicious.\" [2485] \"Give yourself some time.\" [2486] I'm not sure what he meant by that sentence. I asked him why he seemed so troubled. [2487] \"Look at this.\" He handed me the letter he was reading. [2488] Dear Sir,\n[2489] I just thought of something. You asked if there were any obvious features about my husband, and I said no. But I was mistaken. There is actually a scar on the back of his left ear. One time, when he was shaving, our dog lunged at him, and he got a cut from the razor. He had to get several stitches. It was a minor incident, so I didn't remember it that day. [2490] Yours sincerely,\n[2491] Melina Rivale\n[2492] \"Her handwriting is elegant,\" I said, \"although I've never been a fan of purple ink. Does the deceased have any scars?\" [2493] \"He does have a scar. Right where she said it was.\" [2494] \"Didn't she see it when she identified the body?\" [2495] Hakaste shook his head. [2496] \"It was covered by his ear. You have to pull it forward to see it.\" [2497] \"Well, that's settled then. It's solid evidence. What else are you worrying about?\" [2498] Hakast sighed, saying the case was like a devil. He asked if I would visit my French or Belgian friend in London. [2499] \"Maybe. What's up?\" [2500] \"I mentioned him to the county police chief, and he said he was impressed \u2013 the Girl Scout murder case [1]. If he's willing to come here, I'd be more than happy to welcome him.\" [2501] \"I'm afraid not,\" I said. \"This man rarely leaves his house.\" [2502] 2\n[2503] At a quarter past twelve, I rang the doorbell at 62 Wilbraham Crescent. Mrs. Lamech opened the door. She barely looked up at me. [2504] \"What is it?\" she said. [2505] \"Can I talk to you? I came here ten days ago. You may not remember.\" [2506] She looked at me closely for a moment, her eyebrows slightly furrowed. [2507] \"You came here, with the detective, didn't you?\" [2508] \"Yes, Mrs. Limehouse. Can I come in?\" [2509] \"Of course. The police are never turned away. If I did turn you away, I'd be in trouble.\" [2510] She led me into the living room, sitting down roughly in a chair facing me. Her voice had sounded sharp before, but today, she looked tired, something she hadn't shown before. [2511] I said:\n[2512] \"It seems quite quiet here today... Your son has gone back to school, hasn't he?\" [2513] \"Yes, completely different.\" [2514] \"I'm not here about the murder case a few days ago, the girl killed in the phone booth. I'm not really involved with the police, you know.\" [2515] She looked somewhat surprised. [2516] \"I thought you were Detective, Lime, right?\" [2517] \"My name is Lime, yes, but I work in a completely different department.\" The lack of enthusiasm disappeared from Mrs. Lyme's expression. She stared at me directly and sternly. [2518] \"Oh,\" she said, \"well, what is it?\" [2519] \"Is your husband still abroad?\" [2520] \"Yes.\" [2521] \"He's been away for a long time, hasn't he, Mrs. Lam? And he's gone to a very far place?\" [2522] \"What do you know about this?\" [2523] \"Well, he's probably gone into the Iron Curtain country, isn't he?\" [2524] She fell silent for a moment, then spoke in a calm, emotionless voice,\n[2525] \"Yes. Yes, that's right.\" [2526] \"Do you know where he went?\" [2527] \"I know a little about it. He wanted me to meet him there.\" [2528] \"He must have been planning this for some time?\" [2529] \"I think so. He only told me recently.\" [2530] \"You don't agree with his ideas, do you?\" [2531] \"I used to, I think. But you know... you already know the details, don't you?\" [2532] \"You might be able to tell us some very useful information.\" [2533] She shook her head. [2534] \"No. I can't. It's not that I don't want to. You know, he's always been vague with me. I don't want to know. I hate all of this! When Michel told me he was planning to leave the country, go through customs, and go to Moscow, I wasn't surprised at all. Later, I had to make my own decision.\" [2535] \"So you decided not to support your husband's plan?\" [2536] \"No, not like that! My opinion is entirely personal. I think there will always be a woman going with him, of course, probably a fanatic. Some women might be extremely fanatical, but I'm not. I've always been a moderate leftist.\" [2537] \"Is your husband involved with the Larkin case?\" [2538] \"I don't know. Maybe. He never tells me these things.\" [2539] She suddenly looked at me intensely. [2540] \"We should clarify things, Mr. Lamb, or Mr. Wolf in sheep's clothing, whoever you are. I love my husband, and maybe I should go to Moscow with him, regardless of whether I agree with his political views. He wants me to take the children. I don't want to! That's just the way it is. So, I decided I have to stay with the children. Whether I'll ever see Michel again, I don't know. He has to choose his way of life, and I have to choose mine. But there's one thing I'm absolutely certain of: after he talked to me about this, I decided to let the children grow up in their own country. They're British. I want them to grow up like ordinary British children.\" [2541] \"I understand.\" [2542] \"That's all I know.\" Mrs. Lame said, standing up. [2543] Her attitude suddenly became much more determined. [2544] \"This must be a difficult choice,\" I said gently, \"I'm sorry for you.\" [2545] \"Me too.\" Perhaps my sympathy touched her. She smiled faintly. [2546] \"Perhaps you are right... I think your job has made you try to find more information from other people's expressions to know their feelings and thoughts. This must have been a fatal blow to me, but I have already gone through the toughest times... Now I have to make plans, what to do, where to go, whether to stay here or go somewhere else. I should look for a job. I used to do secretarial work. Maybe I should enroll in a refresher course to learn shorthand and typing.\" [2547] \"Well, don't work at Cavenish Typewriting and Stenography.\" [2548] \"Why?\" [2549] \"The girls who work there seem to have unfortunate things happen to them.\" [2550] \"If you think I know anything about that, you're wrong. I don't know.\" [2551] I wished her well and left. I didn't learn anything from her. I actually didn't have any high hopes. But someone has to finish the job. I guess. [2552] 3\n[2553] As I was about to leave the gate, I almost bumped into Mrs. McNaughton. She was carrying a shopping bag and swaying as she walked. [2554] \"Let me help you with that,\" I said, reaching out to take the shopping bag from her. She initially held on to it tightly, then leaned forward and looked at me closely, and finally let go. [2555] \"You're the young man from the police station,\" she said. \"I didn't recognize you at first.\" [2556] I carry the shopping bag to her door. She walks beside me, swaying unsteadily. I didn't expect the bag to be so heavy. I wonder what's inside. Pounds of potatoes? [2557] \"Don't ring the doorbell,\" she says. \"The door is not locked.\" [2558] There seems to be no locked doors on Wilbraham Crescent. [2559] \"How's your case going?\" she asks casually. \"He seemed to have married several times.\" [2560] I don't know what she's talking about. [2561] \"Who, I've been away lately,\" I explained. [2562] \"Oh, I see. You're on a case, I mean Mrs. Rival. I went to the trial. Such an ordinary woman. I couldn't help but think she didn't seem very sad about her husband's death.\" [2563] \"She hadn't seen him for fifteen years,\" I explained. [2564] \"Anne and I have been married for twenty years,\" she sighed. \"A long time. Since he stopped teaching at the university, he started a lot of gardening work... It's hard to know what people should do.\" [2565] Just then, Mr. MacNorton came out from the corner of the house, holding a shovel. [2566] \"Oh, you're back, dear. Let me take care of it\u2014\"\n[2567] \"Put it in the kitchen.\" Mrs. McNoughton said slyly, nudging me with her elbow. \"Just some cornflakes, eggs, and watermelon.\" She smiled cheerfully at her husband. [2568] I placed the shopping bag on the kitchen table. It clanged. [2569] Cornflakes, what a farce! My detective instincts kicked in. Underneath the tape, there were three bottles of whiskey. [2570] I now understood why Mrs. McNoughton was sometimes so cheerfully chatty and why she walked with a slight wobble. Perhaps that's why Mr. McNoughton resigned from his job. [2571] It was still early morning for this neighborhood as I walked down New Moon Street towards Albany Road. I met Mr. Brand along the way. Mr. Brand seemed to be in good spirits. He quickly recognized me. [2572] \"Are you okay? How's the case going? I heard they identified the body. He was pretty rough on his wife, I guess. By the way, sorry, you're not from around here, are you?\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[592] I whistled. [593] \"That's really strange. What's going on?\" [594] \"These damn idiots! I mean, I'm one of them too, I think -\" (Dick is a very honest person) \"You must be very careful, otherwise things will get worse. Well, yesterday the clocks were still properly placed in the living room. I let Miss Pemahyu touch them all to see if she was familiar with them. She said she wasn't. Then they came to move the body.\" [595] \"Then what?\" [596] \"I went out to the front gate to check on other things, then came back inside and spoke to Miss Pemarthy in the kitchen, telling her I had to take these clocks and would give her a receipt.\" [597] \"I remember. I heard you say that.\" [598] \"I then told the lady I would arrange for her to be driven home, and I asked you to take her to the car.\" [599] \"Yes.\" [600] \"Even though Miss Pemarthy said she didn't need a receipt, as the clocks weren't hers, I still gave her one. Then I came to find you. I told Edward to carefully pack the clocks in the living room, and bring them here. All the clocks except the cuckoo clock were packed. Of course, there was also the grandfather clock. That's where I made my mistake. I should have been very specific and told them there were four clocks. Edward said he went in immediately and did what I said. But he insisted that there were only three clocks, besides the two fixed ones.\" [601] \"There wasn't much time between all of this,\" I said, \"which means\u2014\"\n[602] \"It could have been Pemahux. She might have wrapped the clock up and taken it directly to the kitchen after I left the house.\" [603] \"But what about the motive?\" [604] \"We need more information. Was there anyone else? Was it the girl who did it?\" [605] I thought for a moment. \"I don't think so. I\u2014\" I stopped suddenly, remembering something. [606] \"So she might have,\" Hakaste said, \"continue. When was this?\" [607] \"Just as we came out, walking towards the police car,\" I said glumly, \"she forgot her gloves. I said 'I'll go get them for you,' but she said 'Oh, I just know where they are. I don't mind going back to the house now, since the body's been moved.' And then she ran back to the house. But she only left for a minute\u2014\"\n[608] \"Did she have her gloves on when she came back to you, or was she holding them?\" [609] I hesitated. \"Yes, yes, I think she was holding them.\" [610] \"Obviously she wasn't,\" Hakaste said, \"otherwise you wouldn't have hesitated.\" [611] \"She probably stuffed them into her bag.\" [612] \"The problem is,\" Hakast accused, \"you're in love with that girl.\" [613] \"I'm not that stupid,\" I argued, defending myself, \"I met her for the first time yesterday afternoon, and this meeting was far from being the romantic encounter you're implying.\" [614] \"I'm not so sure about that,\" Hakaste said, \"not every girl bursts into a young man's arms, screaming for help in the manner of the Victorian era, making the man feel like a hero, a brave protector. You shouldn't be defending that girl anymore. You know, she's already deeply involved in this murder case and has her own problems to deal with.\" [615] \"You mean this frail little girl stabbed a man to death, carefully hid the knife so you detectives wouldn't find it, and then deliberately ran out of the house, pretending to scream and bumped into me?\" [616] \"If you were me, you wouldn't be surprised,\" Hakaste said with a frown. [617] \"Don't you know,\" I said angrily, \"that I'm surrounded by beautiful spy women from all over the world? They all have measurements that could make an American private detective forget about the black whiskey in his drawer. I'm immune to all female temptations.\" [618] \"Everyone has their Waterloo,\" Hakaste said. \"It's just a matter of what type. Hillary Weber seems to be your type.\" [619] \"Anyway, I don't understand why you're so sure to pin this on her.\" [620] Hakaste sighed. [621] \"I'm not pinning it on her, but I have to start somewhere. The body was found in Pema's house. It involves her. It was the girl named Weber who found the body. I don't need to tell you that the first person to find a body is usually the last one to see him alive. Unless the truth comes out, those two women are not off the hook.\" [622] \"By the time I entered that house at a quarter to three, the person was already dead for at least half an hour or more. How do you explain that?\" [623] \"Hilary Weber's lunchtime is from 1:30 to 2:30 p.m.\"\n[624] I glared at him. [625] \"How is the investigation on Kirby going?\" [626] Hakaste's answer was unexpectedly cold: \"Nothing!\" [627] \"What do you mean, nothing?\" [628] \"It means he doesn't exist, there's no such person.\" [629] \"What about the Big City Small Town Insurance Company?\" [630] \"They have nothing to say because there's no such thing. The Big City Small Town Insurance Company doesn't exist. There's no Mr. Kirby, no Danver Street, no 7 or any other house number.\" [631] \"Interesting,\" I said. \"So you're saying his business card is fake, the name, address, and insurance company are all fake?\" [632] \"That's what it seems like.\" [633] \"What an ingenious idea, don't you think?\" [634] Hakaste shrugged. [635] \"All of this is conjecture. Maybe he's creating a false trail, maybe it's a way for him to introduce himself to get into the house to pull a scam. He could be a con artist, a skilled trickster, a collector of trivial facts, or a private detective. We don't know.\" [636] \"But you'll find out.\" [637] \"Well, yes, we'll find out eventually. We took his fingerprints to see if he has a criminal record. If he does, it would be a big step forward in solving the case; if not, it would make the case more difficult.\" [638] \"A private detective,\" I mused. \"I do believe that could be the case when the truth comes out.\" [639] \"So far, we've only had speculation.\" [640] \"When is the interrogation?\" [641] \"Tomorrow. It's just a formality; the real interrogation will be postponed.\" [642] \"What was the autopsy result?\" [643] \"Well, it was a sharp instrument, like a kitchen knife.\" [644] \"Doesn't that favor Miss Pemerton? A blind woman would have a hard time killing a man. She really is blind, isn't she?\" [645] \"Yes, she is blind. We checked, and it's consistent with what she described. She was a math teacher at a rural school in the north, and she went blind sixteen years ago. She received training in Braille, and eventually found a job at the Allenberg Institute.\" [646] \"Maybe she has a mental illness?\" [647] \"Because of her obsession with clocks and insurance agents?\" [648] \"It's simply indescribable, too strange. My strong curiosity makes me say this, 'like Ariadne Oliver at her worst, or the late Gary Gregson at the height of his career...'\"\n[649] \"Go on and express your emotions. You're not the poor wretch in charge of the criminal investigation team. You don't have to flatter the senior police officers or the police commissioner, or anyone else.\" [650] \"Okay! Maybe we can find useful clues from the neighbors.\" [651] \"No telling,\" Hercule Poirot said coldly, \"If the man was killed in the front garden and then the two masked men carried him into the house. No one looked out the window or noticed anything. This isn't the countryside, our luck would be terrible. Wilbraham Crescent is a residential area for the upper class. The women who finish their work at one o'clock are already home. There's not even a handcart on the street \u2013\"\n[652] \"No elderly people sitting by the window all day, sickly?\" [653] \"We wish there were, but that's impossible.\" [654] \"What about number 18 or 20?\" [655] \"Number 18 is occupied by Mr. Waitehouse \u2013 an assistant to Messrs. Gastford and Swinburne, the lawyers, and his sister who comes to take care of him in his spare time. As for number 20, I only know that a woman lives there who owns eighteen cats. I don't like cats \u2013\"\n[656] I told him, being a policeman is no easy task. And then we set off. [657] Chapter 7\n[658] Mr. Walters paced up and down the steps of 18 Wilbraham Crescent, anxiously looking back at his sister. [659] \"Are you sure you're right?\" Mr. Walters said. [660] Miss Walters huffed in anger. [661] \"I really don't understand what you mean, James.\" [662] Mr. Waltershouse looked apologetic. He must have often expressed regret, as his face seemed to bear the traces of such an expression. [663] \"Well, I just thought, dear, of what happened next door yesterday\u2026\"\n[664] Mr. Waltershouse was preparing to go to his law firm. He was a gentlemanly man with gray hair, slightly hunched, and pale complexion, not pink, but not sickly looking either. [665] Miss Waltershouse was tall and slender, a woman who was serious and intolerant of anyone who was not serious. [666] \"James, just because someone was murdered next door, does that mean I have a reason to be murdered today?\" [667] \"Well, Edith,\" said Mr. Warthouse, \"it depends on the kind of person the killer is?\" [668] \"Do you really think, James, that someone was wandering up and down Wilbraham Crescent, looking for a house to murder someone in? It's sacrilege.\" [669] \"Sacrilege, Edith?\" Mr. Warthouse was astonished. He would never dare say such a thing. [670] \"The Passover memories,\" said Miss Warthouse, \"remind you that it comes from the Bible.\" [671] \"I think that's a bit of a stretch, Edith,\" said Mr. Walterhouse. [672] \"I should have been thinking about who might come here to try and murder me,\" said Miss Walterhouse, becoming quite agitated. [673] Her brother pondered silently, the idea of someone trying to murder her seemed quite impossible. If he were choosing a victim, he would never choose his sister. If someone did attempt it, the assailant would likely be beaten back by a poker or door latch, covered in blood, and handed over to the police in such a humiliating and unusual circumstance. [674] \"I just mean,\" he said, looking more apologetic, \"yes, there have been some unsavory characters around here.\" [675] \"We know very little about what's been happening,\" said Miss Walterhouse. \"Rumors are spreading all over the place. This morning, Mrs. Black said some strange things.\" [676] \"I hope so,\" Mr. Watrous said, glancing at his watch. He had no intention of listening to the idle gossip from the maids. His sister never wasted time debunking such sensational stories; she only took a moment of amusement from them. [677] \"Some people say,\" Miss Watrous said, \"that this man was an accountant or a trustee at Allerton College, and he came to Miss Pembroke to inquire about some discrepancies in the accounts.\" [678] \"And Miss Pembroke killed him?\" Mr. Watrous seemed amused. \"A blind woman? Are you sure\u2014\"\n[679] \"She quietly wrapped a wire around his neck and strangled him,\" Miss Watrous said. \"He didn't suspect anything, you know. Who would suspect a blind woman? I don't believe it,\" she continued. \"I'm sure Miss Pembroke is a woman of excellent character. We may disagree on many things, but that doesn't mean I consider her a suspect. I just think her views are narrow and stubborn. There are other issues besides education. All these new grammar schools, they look so peculiar, with classrooms made of glass. You might think they were built for growing cucumbers or tomatoes. I'm convinced they're not suitable for children during the summer months. Mrs. Hazes told me herself that her daughter Susan doesn't like the new classrooms at the school. She said because of these glass windows, you always want to look outside, and you can't concentrate on your studies.\" [680] \"Oh, dear!\" Mr. Watrous said, glancing at his watch again. \"Well, I'm afraid I'll be late. Goodbye, dear. Take care of yourself. Remember to lock the door!\" [681] Miss Watrous sniffed disdainfully as her brother left. She closed the door and started up the stairs. She paused to think, walked over to her golf bag, and took out a nine-iron, placing it in a prominent spot near the front door. \"Well,\" Miss Watrous said, satisfied