turned away a few reporters who came asking questions.\" [694] \"You turned them away?\" [695] \"Of course.\" [696] \"You did the right thing,\" Hakaste said. \"Of course, they like to sneak back in, but I'm sure you can handle it with your abilities.\" [697] For such praise, Miss Houseworth allowed herself a hint of pleasure. [698] \"I hope you don't mind us asking you the same questions,\" Hakaste said. \"But if you saw anything that could be helpful to us, I'm sure we would be grateful for your information. At that time, I think you were in the house?\" [699] \"I don't know when the murder happened,\" Miss Houseworth said. [700] \"We think it was between 1:30 and 2:30 PM.\" [701] \"I was in the house, yes, indeed.\" [702] \"Where was your brother?\" [703] \"He doesn't come for lunch. Who was murdered? The local papers haven't mentioned anything.\" [704] \"A stranger?\" [705] \"It seems so now.\" [706] \"You're not telling me that for Miss Pemerton he's a stranger too?\" [707] \"Miss Pemerton told us very confidently that she had no appointment with this particular guest, and she didn't know who he was.\" [708] \"She couldn't be so sure,\" said Miss Watrous. \"She can't see.\" [709] \"We described the person's appearance to her in great detail.\" [710] \"What did he look like?\" [711] Mr. Huckleberry took a photograph from the envelope and handed it to her. [712] \"That's the man,\" he said. \"Do you recognize him?\" [713] Miss Warton looked at the photo. \"No, no... I'm sure I've never seen him before. Oh dear. He looks like a very respectable person.\" [714] \"He does look respectable,\" said the detective. \"He looks like a lawyer or a businessman.\" [715] \"Yes, that's true. There's nothing about this photo that suggests any misfortune. He looks like he's sleeping.\" [716] Hakast didn't tell her that this photo was chosen from among many others, the one that was least likely to cause discomfort. [717] \"Death can sometimes be a peaceful thing,\" he said. \"I think this particular man never expected what was about to happen next.\" [718] \"What did Miss Pembroke have to say about this?\" Miss Walters asked. [719] \"She was very surprised.\" [720] \"That's strange!\" Miss Walters commented. [721] \"Now, please help us out, Miss Walterhouse. If you can remember yesterday, try to think if you saw anything outside the window, or if you happened to be in your garden between twelve-thirty and three o'clock, tell us what happened during that time?\" [722] Miss Walterhouse recalled. [723] \"Yes, I was in the garden... let me think. It should have been before one o'clock. I went into the house from the garden at a quarter to one, washed my hands, and then sat down to lunch.\" [724] \"Did you see Miss Pemahku enter or leave the house?\" [725] \"I think she came back. I heard the creak of the gate. Yes, usually after twelve-thirty.\" [726] \"Did you speak to her?\" [727] \"Oh, no. It was just the sound of the gate that made me look up. That's when she usually comes home. I think she finished her class.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 7:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[2971] He summed up finally: \"Damn, damn, damn!\" [2972] \"Have you investigated... others?\" [2973] His answer was very quick. [2974] \"Miss Pembroke was in London yesterday. She had some business for the college and took the 7:40 train back to Crofton.\" [2975] \"Listen, Hercule, I have something to tell you. There is a witness who can testify. On September 9th, a laundry van stopped in front of 19 Wilbraham Crescent at 1:35 p.m. The driver placed a large basket at the back door of the house. It was a particularly large basket.\" [2976] \"The laundry shop? Which laundry shop?\" [2977] \"Snowflake Laundry. You know it?\" [2978] \"Not really. New laundry shops keep popping up. The name of this one sounds pretty ordinary.\u201d\n[2979] \"Well, check it out. A man in a car brought the basket to the house door\u2014\u201d\n[2980] Harkness suddenly became more alert. [2981] \"Is this made up, Colin?\" [2982] \"No. I told you I have a witness. Check it out, Dick. Quickly.\" [2983] As he tried to question me further, I hung up the phone. [2984] I stepped out of the phone booth and checked my watch. I had a lot to do. I didn't want Hakaste interfering with me while I did these things. I wanted to arrange my future life myself. [2985] Chapter Twenty-eight\n[2986] Colin Lamb's Narrative\n[2987] 1\n[2988] On the night of the fifth day, I arrived in Crofton. I went to the Clarendon Hotel and got a room. I went to bed and slept through the night, exhausted from the previous evening. [2989] I asked the waiter to bring me coffee, toast, and the day's newspaper. Along with them came a square envelope with the word \"To Open\" written in the top left corner. [2990] I opened it with curiosity. The envelope was thick and expensive-looking, with neatly printed words on it. [2991] After flipping it over and over again, I finally opened it. [2992] Inside was a sheet of paper with only a few large words:\n[2993] Curlew Hotel, 11:30\n[2994] Room 413\n[2995] (Knock three times!) [2996] I stared at the letter, flipping it back and forth \u2013 what did it mean? [2997] I noticed the room number, 413, which matched the time on the clock. A coincidence? Or intentionally arranged? [2998] I wanted to call Mallard Inn. Then I thought about calling Dick Hakastad. But I ended up not calling anyone. [2999] My lazy mood disappeared completely. I got up, shaved, washed my face, dressed, and walked along the road to Mallard Inn, arriving right on time. [3000] The best season for summer travel had ended. There weren't many people in the hotel. [3001] I didn't go to the front desk. I took the elevator directly to the fourth floor and walked down the corridor to Room 413. [3002] I stood at the door for a moment, then knocked three times, suddenly feeling stupid...\n[3003] A voice came from inside, \"Come in.\" [3004] I turned the door handle, and the door was not locked. I walked in and suddenly stopped. [3005] I saw the person in the world I least wanted to see. [3006] Hercule Poirot sat facing me, smiling warmly. \"Surprise, isn't it?\" he said. \"But I hope it's a pleasant one for you.\" [3007] \"Poirot, you old fox,\" I exclaimed. \"How did you get here?\" [3008] \"I came in a Mercedes-Benz luxury car. It was very comfortable.\" [3009] \"But what are you doing here?\" [3010] \"It's quite distressing. They insist, strongly insist, on renovating my apartment. What can I do? Where can I go?\" [3011] \"Many places,\" I said coldly. [3012] \"Perhaps, but my doctor suggested I go to the seaside, saying the air would be better for me.\" [3013] \"Those considerate doctors discovered where you wanted to go and conveniently suggested it to you!\" I waved the letter I had received. [3014] \"Of course, who else could it be?\" [3015] \"Your room number is 413. Is that a coincidence?\" [3016] \"It's not a coincidence. I specifically requested it.\" [3017] \"Why?\" [3018] Poirot tilted his head to the side and winked at me. [3019] \"It seems quite appropriate.\" [3020] \"So, what about knocking three times?\" [3021] \"I couldn't help myself. If I could have added a sprig of rosemary, it would have been even better. I was going to cut my finger and leave a bloody handprint on the door, but I should stop! I might get infected.\" [3022] \"I think you're getting senile,\" I said coldly. \"I should go buy you a balloon and a stuffed bunny toy this afternoon.\" [3023] \"I don't think you enjoy this surprise. You didn't show any pleasure, and you didn't seem happy to see me.\" [3024] \"Do you expect these?\" [3025] \"Why not? Well, let's get serious now. I have some advice, and I hope it helps you. I've already called the county police commissioner, and he was very friendly. Now, I'm waiting for your friend, Detective Hercule Poirot.\" [3026] \"What are you going to tell him?\" [3027] \"I was thinking that the three of us should have a conversation.\" [3028] I laughed at him. He might call it a conversation, but I knew who would be the one doing all the talking. [3029] Hercule Poirot! [3030] 2\n[3031] Hercule Poirot arrived. We introduced ourselves and greeted each other, then sat down together in a friendly manner, with Dick occasionally stealing glances at Poirot, as if studying a new and intriguing animal in a zoo. I couldn't help but wonder if he had ever encountered someone like Hercule Poirot before. [3032] After exchanging pleasantries, Hercule Poirot cleared his throat and began to speak. [3033] \"I suppose, Poirot sir,\" he said carefully, \"you want to know, um, the whole plan? It's not an easy thing. He hesitated. \"The Chief Constable has asked me to do everything I can to cooperate with you. But you must know there are difficulties, and there are many unanswered questions. However, since you came all the way here\u2014\"\n[3034] Poirot interrupted him, looking rather aloof. [3035] \"I came here,\" he said, \"because my apartment in London needs to be renovated.\" [3036] I burst out laughing, and Poirot gave me a reproachful look. [3037] \"Mr. Poirot, you didn't need to come all the way here, nor did you need to investigate anything,\" I said. \"He always insists that he can know everything just by sitting in an armchair. But is that really true, Poirot? Otherwise, why did you come here?\" [3038] Poirot answered solemnly. [3039] \"I said that there was no need to bring in bloodhounds, large dogs, and tracking dogs to sniff around. But I admit that a dog is necessary for tracking. A good hunting dog, my friend. A good hunting dog.\" [3040] He turned to the detective, twirling his small mustache with a smug expression. [3041] \"Let me tell you,\" he said, \"I don't dote on dogs like the British do. I can live without them. However, I accept your idea about dogs. People love their dogs, pamper them, and brag to their friends about how clever and alert their dog is. The same goes for dogs. They adore their owners. They indulge their owners! They flatter their owners, making them feel very clever. So, even if people don't really want to go for a walk, they'll get up and take their dog for a stroll because their dog loves it. Similarly, the dog will go to great lengths to please its owner and fulfill its owner's wishes.\" [3042] \"My dear young friend, Mr. Colin, came to me, not because he had his own problems to solve. He is confident that he can solve them himself, or, I guess, already has. He was worried that I had nothing to do and was lonely, so he brought me a problem that needed solving, thinking it would interest me and give me a boost of energy. He challenged me, provoked me, and often told me what I could do. Just sitting in my chair, solving the problem in the right circumstances. I suspect this was a deliberate scheme, but with no malice. He wanted to tell everyone and prove to me that it's not easy. You wanted to mock me. That's all! I don't blame you. What I want to say is that you don't understand your Hercule Poirot.\" [3043] He puffed out his chest and twirled his small mustache. [3044] I looked at him, smiling broadly. [3045] \"Well, then,\" I said, \"give us the answer if you know it.\" [3046] Hercule Poirot looked at him suspiciously. [3047] \"You said you know who killed the man at 19 Wilbraham Crescent, right?\" [3048] \"Of course.\" [3049] \"And you know who killed Ingrid Bret?\" [3050] \"Yes.\" [3051] \"Do you know the identity of the deceased?\" [3052] \"I can find out.\" [3053] Hercule Poirot's face showed an incredulous expression. He maintained a polite demeanor, but his voice was filled with doubt. [3054] \"I'm sorry, Mr. Poirot, you claim to know who killed these three people. Is that true?\" [3055] \"Yes.\" [3056] \"So you have solved the case already?\" [3057] \"Not yet.\" [3058] \"It seems you have been speculating all this time.\" I said bluntly. [3059] \"I won't argue with you over trivial matters, dear Collier. I just want to say that I know!\" [3060] Hercule sighed. [3061] \"But you understand, Mr. Poirot, I must have evidence.\" [3062] \"Of course, but from the resources you have at your disposal, it is possible for you to obtain evidence.\" [3063] \"I cannot guarantee that.\" [3064] \"Well, Inspector. If you know, truly know, isn't that the first step? Will you continue?\" [3065] \"Maybe not.\" Hercule sighed. \"There are still some people out there who should be in prison, and we both know it.\" [3066] \"But that's just a few, not\u2014\"\n[3067] I interjected. [3068] \"Well. Well. You know... let us know now!\" [3069] \"I find you still suspecting me. But first, let me explain: being 'certain' means that when the right method is found, everything will fall into place. You won't find another way.\" [3070] \"For God's sake,\" I said, \"continue! I agree with what you said.\" [3071] Poirot settled into a more comfortable position and motioned for the inspector to pour more wine into his glass. [3072] \"There's something you must know, my friend. To solve a problem, you must first find the facts. So, you need a dog, a hound, that can bring things one by one -\"\n[3073] \"To its master,\" I said, \"agreed.\" [3074] \"A man cannot simply sit in a chair and solve a case by reading the news in the newspaper. Because facts must be true, and newspaper information is rarely accurate. They say something happened at four o'clock, but it was actually four minutes past. They say a man has an older sister named Elizabeth, but it's actually his sister-in-law named Alexandra, and so on. But, Colin, I have a dog with extraordinary abilities. This ability, I can say, makes it excel in its work. It has an extraordinary memory. It can repeat, even days-old conversations. It can accurately repeat the content of a conversation, more precisely, the things that are etched in its mind, unlike us who tend to forget. It does not describe vaguely. It will not say, 'The postman came at 11:20 a.m.,' but will describe specific details, like, the front door rang with a knock, someone with a letter in hand entered the house. All these are important. It means that it can hear and see everything like I am present at the scene.\" [3075] \"But this loyal dog failed to make the necessary inferences?\" [3076] \"So, for now, I have these facts, as if 'being there'. That's your wartime term, isn't it? 'Put someone in the picture'. When Colin finished telling the story, what first struck me was the incredible plot. Four clocks, each one an hour ahead of time, all the people brought into the house not recognizing the owner. We cannot, forever, believe what people say until these words are verified.\" [3077] \"Your thoughts are the same as mine.\" Hercule agreed. [3078] \"A dead man lay on the floor, a respectable-looking middle-aged man. No one knew who he was. In his pocket there was a business card with the name R.H. Curry, 7 Denver Street, Big City Small Town Insurance. But there was no Big City Small Town Insurance, no Denver Street 7, and it seemed there was no Mr. Curry either. This was a useless piece of evidence, but it was evidence. Now we analyze further. Obviously, at twenty minutes to two, the Typewriting Agency received a phone call from a Miss Mylees Pemmerton who requested a stenographer to be sent to Wilbraham Crescent 19 at three o'clock, specifically requesting Miss Hillar Webber to go. Miss Webber was sent, arriving around three o'clock, following the instructions and entering the living room to find a dead man on the floor, then screaming and running out. She ran into the arms of a young man.\" [3079] Poirot stopped and looked at me. I bowed forward. [3080] \"I bumped into this young hero,\" I said. [3081] \"You see,\" Poirot continued, \"even you, when you tell this story, cannot help but adopt a ridiculous and exaggerated tone. The whole thing is so dramatic and fantastical, it's completely unbelievable. It's something that could only happen in a novel by Agatha Christie. I was reading a series of detective novels when my young friend came to me with this story. They used all sorts of tricks in their works over the past sixty years. Very interesting. One might almost think that real crimes are imitating the plots of novels. That is, if I find a dog that doesn't bark when it should, I say to myself, 'Ha! A Sherlock Holmes mystery!' Similarly, if a body is found in a completely sealed room, I would naturally say, 'Ha! A Dixon Carr case!' And then there's Mrs. Oliver. If I had found it - even then I wouldn't say much. Do you understand what I mean? This case is so absurd that people will immediately feel, 'This book has nothing to do with real life. Everything is unreal.' But it's no use, because it's a fact. This happened. It's infuriating, isn't it?\" [3082] Hercule Poirot did not analyze it this way, but he agreed with this point of view, nodding his head. Poirot continued:\n[3083] \"This is exactly the opposite of the way in which Chesterton's novels are written. 'Where would you hide a leaf? In the forest. Where would you hide a pebble? On the beach.' Here there is time travel, fantasy, and legend! I said to myself, try to imitate Chesterton. 'Where could a middle-aged woman hide her fading beauty?' I didn't answer. 'Hidden among other aging faces.' That's completely wrong. She was hidden under her makeup, under her lipstick and mascara, wrapped in luxurious fur, surrounded by jewels around her neck, and dangling earrings at her ears. Do you understand?\" [3084] \"Hmm...\" the detective covered his ignorance. [3085] \"Because of these costumes, you know, people are attracted to the high fashion on her, and they pay attention to the fur, the jewelry, the headwear. They don't pay any attention to the woman's face itself! So I said to myself, to my friend Colin, because this murder case is so full of strange designs that it distracts people's attention, so it must be a simple case. Am I right?\" [3086] \"You're right,\" I said. \"But I still don't understand how you knew who the killer was.\" [3087] \"So you'll have to wait. So, now, instead of looking at the designs of the case, let's analyze the essence. A man was killed. Why was he killed? Who was he? The answer to the first question is clearly dependent on the second. Until you get the correct answers to both questions, you can't proceed any further. He could have been a blackmailer, or a swindler, or a husband who was detested by his wife, whose existence posed a terrible threat to her. He could have been just an ordinary person. More and more people are saying that he was just an ordinary, respected wealthy man. Suddenly, I thought to myself, 'Since it should be a simple murder case, then let's do it this way. Let this man be what he seems to be\u2014a wealthy and respected middle-aged man.'\" [3088] \"Hmm...\" The inspector merely replied with a polite \"hmm\" and stopped. [3089] \"So he was this kind of person, an ordinary, pleasant middle-aged man, and his disappearance must have been necessary for 'someone.' Who, then? We can narrow down the search. Understand the usual situation\u2014Miss Pemmerton and her habits, Cavendish Typewriting Agency, and the girl named Sheila Webb who works there. So I told my friend Colin, 'Go talk to the neighbors. Find out some things. Their backgrounds. But most importantly, get into the conversation. Because in this conversation where you're not just looking for answers to questions, in this casual conversation, things will be inadvertently revealed. When the topic becomes dangerous for them, people enter a state of self-preservation, but once they feel relaxed, they will inadvertently say the truth. This will greatly advance the case.'\" [3090] \"A clever method,\" I said. \"Unfortunately, it didn't work in this case.\" [3091] \"But, dear, it worked.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[453] \"Alright, alright,\" Dick said. \"You win.\" [454] Chapter 5\n[455] \"Mom!\" O'Neil Courtin stopped and said, sliding a small metal rocket model up and down the windowpane to simulate the rocket's journey through space and into the orbit of Venus, \"Mom, what are you thinking?\" [456] Mrs. Cortin, a stern-faced woman, was busy washing ceramic dishes in the sink and didn't respond. [457] \"Mom, a police car is parked outside our house.\" [458] \"Didn't I tell you not to lie anymore, O'Neil?\" Mrs. Cortin said, roughly placing the dishes on the drying rack. \"Do you remember what I told you before?\" [459] \"I'm not lying,\" O'Neil insisted, \"there really is a police car, and two men got out of it.\" [460] Mrs. Cortin suddenly turned to her child. [461] \"What have you done?\" she demanded. \"Bringing shame and disgrace upon us? That's what you've done!\" [462] \"Of course not,\" Oney said. \"I didn't do anything.\" [463] \"It's those Alf and his gang,\" Mrs. Korting said. \"He and his cronies. A bunch of thugs! I told you, your father told you, they're no good. They always get into trouble. First, they'll be brought before the juvenile court, then sent to a juvenile detention center. I don't want you to turn out like that, do you hear?\" [464] \"They're at the front door,\" Oney said loudly. [465] Mrs. Korting put down her work in the sink and walked over to the window next to her child. [466] \"Hmm,\" she grumbled. [467] Just then, there was a knock at the door. Mrs. Cording quickly wiped her hands with a tea towel and went through the hallway to open the door. She looked at the two men on the doorstep with contempt and suspicion. [468] \"Are you Mrs. Cording?\" the taller man asked politely. [469] \"Yes,\" said Mrs. Cording. [470] \"Can I come in? I'm Detective Hakaste.\" [471] Mrs. Corton reluctantly moved back. She opened the door and gestured for the detective to come in. The room was spotlessly clean, giving the impression that few visitors were welcomed, and this was indeed the case. [472] Enoch, curious, came out of the kitchen and quietly leaned against the door. [473] \"Your son?\" Detective Inspector Hakast asked. [474] \"Yes,\" Mrs. Corton said, defending him with a defiant tone, \"He's a good boy, not what you think.\" [475] \"I believe him to be,\" Detective Inspector Hakast said politely. [476] Mrs. Cottington's hostility subsided. [477] \"I came to ask you a few questions about 19 Wilbraham Crescent. I understand you work there.\" [478] \"I haven't denied that,\" Mrs. Cottington said, still not completely recovered from her previous emotions. [479] \"You work for Miss Millechamp.\" [480] \"Yes, Miss Millechamp is a very kind lady.\" [481] \"Blind,\" said Inspector Hakast. [482] \"Yes, poor thing. But perhaps you didn't know, she has a pair of magic hands, and she can easily cope with life through touch. She can even go out on the street and cross the road by herself. She's different from some people I know who are always whining about nothing.\" [483] \"Do you usually work there in the morning?\" [484] \"Yes. I get there around nine-thirty to ten, and leave at twelve, or whenever I finish my work.\" [485] \"On the contrary,\" thought the inspector, thinking of the four clocks. [486] Mrs. Corten looked at him puzzled. [487] \"What happened?\" she asked. [488] \"A man was found dead in the living room of 19 Wilbraham Crescent this afternoon,\" the detective said. [489] Mrs. Corten's eyes widened. Eunice Corten twisted and turned in excitement, saying \"Oh\" with her mouth open, but then closed it again when she realized someone might see her. [490] \"Dead?\" Mrs. Corten couldn't believe it. And even more puzzling was, \"In the living room?\" [491] \"Yes, he was stabbed,\" he said. [492] \"You mean someone killed him?\" she asked. [493] \"Yes, he was killed,\" he replied. [494] \"Who did it?\" Mrs. Courting pressed. [495] \"That's all we know so far,\" said Huckleberry. \"We thought maybe you could help us.\" [496] \"I had nothing to do with the murder,\" Mrs. Kittredge said firmly. [497] \"Not about the murder, but there are one or two things we'd like to ask you. Did anyone come to see you this morning?\" [498] \"I don't remember anyone. No, not today. Who was he?\" [499] \"He was about sixty, wearing a well-dressed dark suit. He may have claimed to be an insurance agent for a company.\" [500] \"I wouldn't let him in,\" Mrs. Kittredge said. \"There's no insurance agent, and no one selling vacuum cleaners or encyclopedias. Pembroke wouldn't tolerate them, and neither would I.\" [501] \"According to the information on the card he brought, his name is Mr. Corley. Have you ever heard of that name?\" [502] \"Corley? Corley?\" Mrs. Cortlandt shook her head. \"Sounds like an Indian name.\u201d She guessed. [503] \"Oh, no,\" Hercule said, \"he's not Indian.\" [504] \"Who found him, Miss Pemerton?\" [505] \"A young lady, a stenographer, who came here by mistake, thinking she was sent to work for Miss Pemerton. She found the body. Miss Pemerton came home almost at the same time.\" [506] Mrs. Huttington sighed deeply. [507] \"How could this happen,\" she said, \"how could this happen!\" [508] \"We would like to ask you to look at the body of this man, at your convenience, and tell us if you have seen him on Wilbraham Crescent or if he ever visited Miss Pemarhu. Miss Pemarhu is quite certain he never came to her house. There are a few points I would like to ask you about, can you remember how many clocks there were in her sitting room?\" [509] Mrs. Huttington hesitated not at all. [510] \"The big clock was in the corner, they called it a grandfather clock; and there was a cuckoo clock on the wall, with a bird that would pop out and say 'cuckoo.' It didn't startle you, did it?\" She added quickly, \"I never touched any of them, never. Miss Pemarhu liked to wind them herself.\" [511] \"Those two clocks are all right,\" said the detective, reassuring her. \"Are you sure there were only those two in the room this morning?\" [512] \"Of course. Should there have been more?\" [513] \"For example, was there a square silver clock, a traveling clock? Or a gilded little clock on a shelf, or a ceramic clock with a patterned face, or a leather clock with the word 'Rosemary' written on one side?\" [514] \"Certainly not. I have never seen any of those things.\" [515] \"If they were there, you would have noticed them, wouldn't you?\" [516] \"Certainly.\" [517] \"These four clocks are all faster than the cuckoo clock and the grandfather clock by an hour and a half.\" [518] \"Maybe they're foreign clocks,\" Mrs. Cochran said. \"My husband and I went on a trip to Switzerland and Italy, and their time was an hour ahead of ours. It must have something to do with the Common Market. I'm not in favor of the Common Market, and neither is Mr. Cochran. I think British things are good enough for me.\" [519] Detective Hercule Poirot didn't want to discuss politics. [520] \"Can you tell me the exact time you left Miss Pemuk's house this morning?\" [521] \"Twelve fifteen, that's the time,\" Mrs. Kettle said. [522] \"Was Miss Pemuk at home then?\" [523] \"No, she wasn't. She hasn't come back yet. She usually comes back between twelve and twelve thirty, but sometimes she doesn't.\" [524] \"When did she leave home?\" [525] \"Before I got there. I got there at ten o'clock.\" [526] \"Thank you, Mrs. Cottington.\" [527] \"These clocks do look rather strange,\" said Mrs. Cottington. \"Perhaps Miss Pembroke went to an auction market. They're antiques, aren't they? Based on your description, they seem like it.\" [528] \"Does Miss Pembroke often go to auction markets?\" [529] \"Four months ago, she bought a roll of mountain wool carpet at an auction market. The quality was very good. She told me it was very cheap. She also bought some silk curtains. Although they needed to be trimmed a bit, they looked brand new.\" [530] \"But she doesn't usually buy small antiques, paintings, or ceramics like that at auction markets, does she?\" [531] Mrs. Kudin shook her head. [532] \"I haven't seen her do that since I've known her, but of course, it's hard to say about auction markets, isn't it? I mean, you lose your self-control there. When you get home, you'll be thinking, 'What am I doing buying these things?' I've bought six jam jars. I keep thinking I could have gotten them cheaper. The cups and plates are the same. I could have gotten better ones at the Tuesday market.\" [533] She shook her head slightly. Detective Hakaste felt he had no more questions to ask and left. Then Oona began to reflect on the topic they had just discussed. [534] \"Murder! Oh my!\" said Oona. [535] The idea of conquering space was immediately overshadowed by the exciting conversation of the day. [536] \"Miss Pemaroo couldn't have done that, could she?\" he ventured to analyze. [537] \"Don't talk nonsense,\" said his mother. An idea flashed through her mind. \"I don't know whether I should tell him\u2014\"\n[538] \"Tell him what, mom?\" [539] \"It's none of your business,\" said Mrs. Kettlety. \"In fact, there's nothing to tell.\" [540] Chapter Six\n[541] Colin Lumb's Narrative\n[542] 1\n[543] After having two half-cooked steaks and a pint of beer outside, Dick Hakaste satisfiedly sighed and announced that he felt better, then said,\n[544] \"Dead insurance agents, strange clocks, and screaming girls, go to hell! Let's talk about you, Colin. I thought you disappeared from the world. I didn't expect to see you wandering around Croton's back street. I can assure you that marine biologists won't find their calling in Croton.\" [545] \"Do you look down on marine biology, Dick? It's a useful profession. But it's so boring to talk about that people get annoyed when you mention it, so you don't need to explain further.\" [546] \"No chance to show yourself off, eh?\" [547] \"You forget,\" I said coldly, \"I'm an oceanographer. I have a degree in the subject from Cambridge University. Not very good, but at least it's a degree. It's an interesting profession, and someday I'll go back to it.\" [548] \"Of course, I know what you've been doing,\" Hackerst said. \"Congratulations. The trial of Rackin is next month, isn't it?\" [549] \"Yes.\" [550] \"It's amazing how he managed to smuggle the information out. I thought someone would suspect him.\" [551] \"No one would suspect him,\" I said. [552] \"He must be smart,\" Dick commented. [553] I shook my head. [554] \"No, I don't think he's smart. In fact, I think he just did what he was told to do. He could get his hands on very important documents. He took them out with him, had them photographed, gave them back, and then they were sent back to their original location the same day. Everything was arranged in a very tight and compact manner. He developed a habit of having lunch in different places. We thought he always hung his coat in the same place, but it was actually a coat identical to his, and the man wearing it was never the same person. The coat was switched, but the man who switched it never spoke to Rake, and Rake never spoke to him. We wanted to know more technical details. This plan was flawless, and the timing was precise. This person is indeed very smart.\" [555] \"That's why you're still hanging around the Portsmouth Naval Base, right?\" [556] \"Yes, we know the location in the naval base and the location in London. We know when and where Rackin collects his salary and how he collects it. But there's a gap between these two points. There's a subtle and delicate organization between them. That's where we want to know more, because that's where the brainpower comes into play. There's a great command center here, a route that will make you doubtful not once, but seven or eight times. It's a superb plan.\" [557] \"Why did Rackin do all this?\" Hakast asked curiously, \"For political ideals? Promoting individualism? Or for quick money?\" [558] \"He's not an idealist,\" I said. \"I think it's 'just for money'.\" [559] \"If you had started from that angle earlier, wouldn't you have found him faster? He must have spent the money. He doesn't save money.\" [560] \"Oh, that's right! He spent all the money. In fact, I was a bit hasty in dealing with him.\" [561] Hakast nodded understandingly. [562] \"I see. You fell once and then gradually used him. Right?\" [563] \"More or less. Before we found him, he had already taken out some valuable intelligence, so we let him leak more intelligence, obviously very valuable. In my workplace, we sometimes have to act like idiots.\" [564] \"I don't like your job, Colin,\" Hakast said thoughtfully. [565] \"It's not the kind of exciting job people think it is,\" I said. \"In fact, it's usually monotonous, but not just boring. Now people have a feeling that there is nothing that is truly secret. We know their secrets, and they know ours. Our agents are often their agents, and their agents are often ours. In the end, whoever is the double agent, it becomes a nightmare! Sometimes, I think, everyone knows each other's secrets, but they set up conspiracies and pretend not to know.\" [566] \"I see what you mean,\" Dick said kindly. [567] He looked at me curiously. [568] \"I know why you're still in Potterbury. But Croton is ten miles away from Potterbury.\" [569] \"Actually, my real purpose is,\" I said, \"New Moon Street.\" [570] \"New Moon Street?\" Hakast looked puzzled. [571] \"Yes. Or, the moon, the new moon, the rising moon, etc. I started my exploration from Portbury. There's a pub called 'New Moon' there. I spent a lot of time there. It sounds a bit crazy. Then there's 'Moon and Stars', 'Rising Moon', 'Happy Scythe', and 'Cross and Crescent' \u2013 that one's in a place called Simde. Nothing came of it. So I gave up on the moon pubs and started studying crescent-shaped streets. There are a few of them in Portbury. There's Lancelbury Crescent, Addington Crescent, Lifemide Crescent, and Victoria Crescent.\" [572] Suddenly, I saw a confused expression on Dick's face and started laughing. [573] \"Don't look so bewildered, Dick. There's something real waiting for me to start.\" [574] I took out my wallet and pulled out a piece of paper from it, handing it to him. It was a hotel stationery paper, on which a sketch was drawn. [575] \"This is something a boy named Hambley had in his wallet. He put a lot of effort into the case of Racking. He's a good guy, really good. He was hit by a car in London and died. The driver fled. No one saw the license plate. I don't know what this is, but it's something Hambley hastily wrote down or copied, thinking it was important. It made him think of something? Did he see or hear anything? Maybe something related to the moon or the new moon, and the number 61 and the capital letter M. After his death, this note fell into my hands. I don't know what I'm looking for, but I'm sure I'll find something. I don't know what 61 represents, or what M means. I've been busy for three weeks now, within a radius centered on Portbury. I haven't found anything. Cromwell is within my plans. That's it. To be honest, Dick, I don't have much hope for Cromwell. There's only one New Moon Street there, which is Wilbraham New Moon Street. Before asking you if you found any information that can help me, I'm going to walk along Wilbraham New Moon Street first and see what the number 61 looks like. That's what I did this afternoon, but I couldn't find number 61.\" [576] \"I told you, 61 is occupied by a local architect.\" [577] \"That's not what I'm looking for. Are there any foreigners living there?\" [578] \"There might be. Many people do these days. If there are, they'll be listed. I'll check for you tomorrow.\" [579] \"Thank you, Dick.\" [580] \"I'll be doing routine inquiries to the neighbors on either side of 19 tomorrow. Asking if they've seen anyone go to that house, and so on. I'll go to the house behind 19, the one whose garden connects with 19's. I seem to remember that 61 is right behind 19. If you'd like, I can take you along.\" [581] I eagerly agreed. [582] \"I'll be your RAMS officer, in charge of taking notes.\" [583] We agreed to meet at the police station at 9:30 the next morning. [584] 2\n[585] On the second day, I arrived at the appointed time and found my friend seething with anger. [586] After he sent away his sulking subordinate, I cautiously asked him what had happened. [587] For a moment, Hakaste seemed unable to speak. After a while, he blurted out, \"It's those damn clocks again!\" [588] \"Clocks again? What's the matter this time?\" [589] \"One of them is missing.\" [590] \"Missing? Which one?\" [591] \"The leather travel clock. The one with 'Rosemary' written on it.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIs this the man who came to ask me if I wanted to buy a set of fourteen-volume new encyclopedias? Or the one who came with the latest vacuum cleaner? We have no connection with him. He went to the front garden, where he annoyed my husband, who was planting bulbs. You know, he didn't want to be disturbed, but the man kept talking about his product. You know, how it comes up and down to clean curtains, how it cleans doorsteps, stairs, cushions, and does a thorough cleaning. He almost mentioned everything. Then Angus looked up at him and said, 'Can it plant bulbs?' I must say it made me laugh out loud, as it forced the man to suddenly step back and leave.\" [1120] \"So, you're absolutely sure this man is the one in the photo?\" [1121] \"Well, no, I can't be sure,\" said Mrs. MacNutt, \"because it was a younger man. Let me think. But anyway, I think I've seen this face before. Yes. The more I look at it, the more sure I am that he came here and made me buy something.\" [1122] \"Maybe insurance?\" [1123] \"No, no, not insurance. My husband took care of all that. We are fully insured. No. But anyway, yes, the more I look at this photo\u2026\"\n[1124] Hackett was not encouraged by this, on the contrary, perhaps even worse. From his rich experience, he thought Mrs. MacNutt was too eager to have seen someone related to the murder case. So the longer she looked at the photo, the more certain she would be that she could remember the man in the photo. [1125] He sighed. [1126] \"I think he was driving a big truck,\" said Mrs. McNutt, \"but I can't remember when I saw him. I think it was a bakery truck.\" [1127] \"Did you see him yesterday, Mrs. McNutt?\" [1128] Mrs. McNutt's face fell. She brushed her greasy gray curls back from her forehead. [1129] \"No, no, not yesterday,\" she said, \"at least -\" She stopped, \"I think not.\" Then her face brightened again. \"Maybe my husband will remember.\" [1130] \"Is he home?\" [1131] \"Oh, he's out in the garden,\" she said, pointing out the window. An older man was pushing a wheelbarrow along a path. [1132] \"Perhaps we should go out and talk to him.\" [1133] \"Of course, come this way.\" [1134] She led them through a side door and into the garden. Mr. MacNorton looked flushed and sweaty. [1135] \"These two gentlemen are from the police station, Angus,\" his wife panted. \"They want to ask about the murder at Miss Pemmican's house. Here's a picture of the deceased. Do you know, I'm sure I've seen him before. Was it last week when he came asking if we had any antiques to dispose of? Was it that man?\" [1136] \"Let me see,\" Mr. McNaughton said, \"just hold it up for me. I've got mud on my hands and can't touch anything.\" He addressed Mr. Harkness, \"You know about my gardening, don't you?\" [1137] \"Yes, Mr. Harkness said so,\" Mr. McNaughton grunted. [1138] \"Not Mrs. Lymes, I hope.\" [1139] \"No, it was Mr. Brand.\" [1140] Angus McNaughton snorted. [1141] \"Brand doesn't know what gardening is,\" he said, \"he just digs holes and then plants begonias, geraniums, and Cape tulips. That's not what I call gardening. Those flowers would survive in the park. Are you interested in shrubs, Inspector? Of course, it's not the season for planting shrubs, but I have a couple of shrubs here that you might be surprised to see I've kept alive. It's said that shrubs thrive best in Devonshire and Cornwall.\" [1142] \"I'm afraid I'm not much of a gardener,\" said Mr. Huckleberry. [1143] Mr. McNaughton looked at him as an artist might look at someone who doesn't understand art but knows what they like. [1144] \"Perhaps I should start talking about the unpleasant topic,\" said Mr. Huckleberry. [1145] \"Certainly. It happened yesterday, I was in the garden.\" [1146] \"Really?\" [1147] \"Well, I mean, when I heard that girl's scream, I was here.\" [1148] \"What were you doing?\" [1149] \"Well,\" Mr. MacNoughton said, startled, \"I didn't do anything. In fact, I thought it was those two noisy kids from the Lymeses' house. They're always yelling, screaming, making a racket.\" [1150] \"But the scream came from a different direction, didn't it?\" [1151] \"Yes, if those boys had stayed in their own garden. But they don't, you know. They're always jumping over other people's fences and hedges. They're chasing poor Mrs. Black's cats all over the place. Nobody's watching them, that's the trouble. Their mother's a nice, quiet sort. Of course, when there's no man in the house, the children get out of hand.\" [1152] \"I know Mr. Rimmers often goes abroad.\" [1153] \"He's an engineer, as far as I know,\" Mr. McNaughton said vaguely, \"always on the move. Dam building, or pipelines, or something like that. I'm not criticizing, dear, but I mean this kind of dam building, or laying oil pipelines, or whatever. I don't know the real story. He rushed off to Sweden a month ago, leaving a lot of work for the children's mother\u2014cooking and housework. Well, no wonder they're getting wild. They're not bad at heart, they just need discipline.\" [1154] \"Did you see anything besides hearing the scream? I mean, when did you hear it, by the way?\" [1155] \"I'm not sure. I usually take off my watch when I go into the garden. The other day, the water pipe was broken, and it took me a while to fix it. What time was that, dear? You heard it too, didn't you?\" [1156] \"Probably around half past two, at least half an hour after we had lunch.\" [1157] \"I see. When do you have lunch?\" [1158] \"At half past one,\" Mr. McNaughton said. \"If we're lucky. Our Danish girl doesn't have a good sense of time.\" [1159] \"And then what? Do you take a nap?\" [1160] \"Sometimes. But not today. I need to finish what I started. I cleared up a lot of waste and added it to the compost pile.\" [1161] \"Great, making compost,\" said Hakaste seriously. [1162] Mr. McNuton immediately brightened up. [1163] \"Absolutely right. Nothing beats homemade compost. I've convinced many people of that. Using chemical fertilizers is like self-destruction, isn't it? Let me show you.\" [1164] He enthusiastically pulled Hakaste's arm and pushed the wheelbarrow along the path to the fence separating his garden from No. 19. The compost was surrounded by lilacs, making it look enticing. Mr. McNuton pushed the wheelbarrow to a small shed next to it. The shed was neatly arranged with various tools. [1165] \"You've organized everything so well,\" commented Hakaste. [1166] \"You must take care of your tools,\" McNotton said. [1167] Hakaste closely observed the 19th garden. On the other side of the fence was a path lined with roses, leading to the corner of the house. [1168] \"Did you see anyone in the 19th garden or peering out the window while you were making compost?\" [1169] McNotton shook his head. [1170] \"I didn't see anyone. Sorry I couldn't help you, Detective.\" [1171] \"You know, Angus,\" his wife said, \"I'm sure I saw a shadowy figure hiding in the garden of number 19.\" [1172] \"I don't think you did, dear,\" her husband said firmly. \"I didn't either.\" [1173] \"That woman kept saying she saw this and that, as if she could spot anything,\" Hakaste grumbled as they got back in the car. [1174] \"Do you think she recognized the person in the photo?\" [1175] Hakaste shook his head. \"I doubt it. She's just imagining she's seen him. I know this kind of witness. When I asked her to clarify, she couldn't answer, right?\" [1176] \"That's right.\" [1177] \"Of course, she could have said she saw him on the bus or somewhere else, sitting across from her. But if you ask me, I think it's just her wishful thinking. What do you think?\" [1178] \"I agree.\" [1179] \"We didn't learn anything,\" Hakast sighed. \"Of course, some things seem strange. For example, Mrs. Black, no matter how she tried to associate herself with the cat, shouldn't be completely unaware of her neighbor Miss Pema, which seems impossible. And she was so confused about the murder case, showing no concern.\" [1180] \"She's just a muddled woman.\" [1181] \"No brains!\" Hakaste said. \"When you encounter such a silly woman, fires, thefts, and murders happening around them, they won't even pay attention.\" [1182] \"Her wire mesh fence is quite effective. The Victorian shrubs almost hide everything from view.\" [1183] They returned to the police station. Hakaste grinned at his friend:\n[1184] \"Well, Detective Blue, I can now let you go home.\" [1185] \"No more interviews?\" [1186] \"Not for now. I'll be visiting a few more places later, but not with you.\" [1187] \"All right, then. Thank you for this morning. Will you have my notes typed up? Here's the folder.\" \"You said the interrogation is tomorrow, isn't it? What time?\" [1188] \"Eleven o'clock.\" [1189] \"Good. I'll be back then.\" [1190] \"You're leaving?\" [1191] \"I'm going to London to give a report tomorrow. I can guess who it is.\" [1192] \"Don't say anything.\" [1193] Hakaste grinned. [1194] \"Say hello to that guy for me.\" [1195] \"Also, I might go see an expert,\" said Colin. [1196] \"An expert? What kind of expert? What's going on?\" [1197] \"Nothing, just stupid. Not that kind of expert. He's in your line of work. He's my father's friend, and he's my friend. You know, he loves these kind of things. It excites him. I think he needs a little stimulation.\" [1198] \"Scotland Yard?\" [1199] \"No. A private detective, a friend of my father's, and also a friend of mine. Your bizarre work would be right up his alley. He enjoys these things. It would excite him. I think he needs some stimulation.\" [1200] \"What's his name?\" [1201] \"Hercule Poirot.\" [1202] \"I've heard of him. I thought he was dead.\" [1203] \"He's still alive. But I feel he has nothing to do. It's worse than being dead.\" [1204] \"You're a strange guy, Colin. Having such strange friends. \" \"And you're no different,\" Colin said with a grin. [1205] ***\n[1206] [1] In English, the words \"dam\" (as in a dam) and \"damn\" are pronounced the same. [1207] Chapter 12\n[1208] After sending Colin away, Inspector Hercule looked at the neatly written address on his notepad and nodded. Then he quietly put the notebook back in his pocket and began to deal with the pile of daily files on his desk. [1209] He worked all day. He sent someone out to buy him coffee and sandwiches, received a report from Sergeant Grey - no useful leads were found. No one recognized Mr. Kuri's photo at the train station or bus station. The check of the clothing in the laboratory was also fruitless. The suit was made by a skilled tailor, but the label of the tailor's shop had been cut off. Was the information that Mr. Kuri wanted to hide his identity or the identity of the killer? The detailed report on the deceased's teeth had been distributed to various places, which was likely the most useful clue. It would take some time, but eventually, there would be a result. Unless, Mr. Kuri was a foreigner? Hercule imagined, it was likely that the deceased was French, but his clothes were not French-made, and there were no laundry shop labels to inquire about. [1210] Inspector Hercule was not impatient. Confirming the identity is often a slow process. But in the end, someone would come forward to confirm it, perhaps the laundry shop owner, the dentist, the doctor, or the landlady. Mr. Kuri's photo would be sent to various police stations and would be published in newspapers. Soon, Mr. Kuri's true identity would be discovered. [1211] Meanwhile, there was other work to be done, not just Mr. Kirby's case. Detective Hakast worked non-stop until 5:30 PM. He glanced at his watch again and thought it was a suitable time to visit a few more places. [1212] According to Inspector Gray's report, Ms. Sheila Webber had returned to Cavenish Typewriting Agency and had an appointment with Professor Puddy at the Heron Hotel at 5:00 PM, and she would not leave until after 6:00 PM. [1213] What was the name of the aunt? Mrs. Rotten, Mrs. Rotten. Living at 14 Palmerston Road. He did not drive his police car but chose to walk the shortcut. [1214] It was said that Palmerston Road used to be a lifeless road. Detective Hakast noticed that most of the houses had been converted into apartments or small houses. As he turned the corner, a girl was walking along the sidewalk towards him. She seemed to hesitate for a moment. Detective Hakast was thinking about other things at the time, and he thought she was going to ask him for directions. But if that was the case, why did she change her mind and continue to walk past him? He wondered why he suddenly thought of shoes. Shoes... No, just one shoe. This girl made him feel vaguely familiar. Who was she? Someone he had seen recently... maybe she recognized him and wanted to talk to him? [1215] He stopped and looked back at her. She was walking quickly by now. The problem was, her face had no clear and distinct features, making it hard to remember, unless there was a special reason. She had blue eyes, fair skin, and a slightly open mouth. The mouth. This made him think of something else. What was she doing with her mouth? Speaking? Putting on lipstick? No. He was a bit annoyed with himself. Detective Hakast prided himself on being able to remember people's facial features. He could say that he would never forget the people he had seen in the defendant's and witness's stands, but after all, he didn't just deal with those people. He couldn't remember, for example, every waitress who had served him. He couldn't remember every bus conductor. So he decided not to think about it for now. [1216] He arrives at number 14. The door is half open, and there are four bells beside it with names written underneath. He sees Mrs. Rudd. She lives in the ground-floor apartment. He goes in and rings the doorbell on the left of the hall. After a few minutes, he hears movement. Soon, footsteps are heard from inside. A tall, thin woman opens the door, her black hair disheveled, wearing a large apron, and seems to be out of breath. There is an onion smell coming from the house, obviously from the kitchen. [1217] \"Is this Mrs. Rudd?\" [1218] \"What do you want?\" She looks at him suspiciously and seems a little annoyed. [1219] He thinks she is about forty-five years old and looks a bit like a gypsy. [1220] \"What do you want?\" [1221] \"Could I trouble you for a moment to discuss something?\" [1222] \"Well, what is it? I'm very busy now.\" She spoke quickly, \"Are you a reporter?\" [1223] \"Of course not,\" Detective Hackett said sympathetically, \"I imagine you've been bothered by reporters quite a bit, haven't you?\" [1224] \"Yes, they keep knocking on the door and ringing the bell, asking all sorts of stupid questions.\" [1225] \"That can be quite irritating, I know,\" Detective Hackett said, \"I hope we can help alleviate some of that for you, Mrs. Rutter. I'm Detective Hackett, by the way, in charge of the case that has reporters bothering you. We'll do our best to prevent that from happening, but unfortunately, we can't control the press, you know, they have their own rights.\" [1226] \"It's inconsiderate of them to bother others like that,\" Mrs. Rudd said, \"claiming they have to report the news for the public. I've seen their reports in the newspaper, and they're all full of nonsense. As far as I know, they just make up facts. Please come in, Detective.\" [1227] She stepped back a few steps, and Detective Hakast walked through the doorway. She closed the door. There were several envelopes on the doormat. Mrs. Rudd was about to bend down to pick them up when Detective Hakast politely beat her to it. He glanced at the envelopes, checking the addresses, before handing them over to the hostess. [1228] \"Thank you.\" [1229] She placed them on the table in the hall. [1230] \"Go ahead to the living room, please? I'll be right there. The food in the kitchen is almost ready.\" [1231] She hurried off to the kitchen. Detective Hackett glanced one last time at the letters on the hall table. One was addressed to Mrs. Rotten, and the other two were for Miss R.S. Webber. He followed Mrs. Rotten's directions into the room. It was a small, messy room with old, stained furniture and unidentifiable stains and spots everywhere. There was an eye-catching, expensive Venetian cast glass dish, an abstract model, two colorful velvet cushions, and an exotic-looking large ceramic shallow dish. He thought that either the aunt or the niece had some taste. [1232] Mrs. Rotten returned, panting even more than before.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nSometimes the bus lines are very long, and the Mute Hotel is at the end of the seafront promenade. You don't have anything to leave for Sheila, do you?\" [1347] \"I don't think so.\" [1348] As he walked out, he asked casually, \"By the way, who chose your niece's Christian name, Rosemary and Sheila? Your sister or yourself?\" [1349] \"Sheila is our mother's name. Rosemary was chosen by my sister. It's actually a very unusual name. Fanciful. But my sister doesn't like fancifulness or sentimentality.\" [1350] \"Well, goodnight, Mrs. Luton.\" [1351] As the detective turned into the street from the front gate, he thought, \"Rosemary... Rosemary... because of memories? Romantic memories? Or something quite special?\" [1352] Chapter 13\n[1353] Colin Lamb's Narrative\n[1354] I walked down Charing Cross Road, turning into a maze of streets between New Oxford Street and Covent Garden. There were all sorts of shops you wouldn't expect: antique shops, doll hospitals, ballet shoe shops, and foreign delicatessens. [1355] I resisted the temptation of the various blue or brown glass eyes in the doll hospital and finally arrived at my destination. It was a dimly lit street-side bookstore near the British Museum. There were usually books displayed on the outside tray, ranging from classic novels to old textbooks and all sorts of miscellaneous books, each with a price tag. Some of the books were almost complete in pages, and occasionally there were pristine, unblemished ones. [1356] I squeezed through the doorway. With more and more books piled up on both sides of the entrance, there was a constant risk of collapse, so I had to squeeze through sideways. Once inside, you would find that the books occupied all the space. There were books everywhere, lying flat or standing upright, in all sorts of positions, obviously ready to topple over at the slightest touch. The distance between the shelves was so narrow that it took a lot of effort to walk through them. These piles of books were temporarily placed on shelves or tables. A chair in the corner was surrounded by books, and an old man sat on it, wearing a flat-crowned, rolled-brim hat, with a flat face like a stuffed fish. He looked weary, like someone who had given up fighting for equality. He tried to control the books, but it was clear that the books had controlled him. He was King Canute in the world of books, who chose to withdraw before the tide of books swept over him. This was Mr. Solomon, the bookstore owner. He recognized me, and his cold gaze softened slightly, nodding at me. [1357] \"Do you have any books I want?\" I asked. [1358] \"You'll have to go up and look for yourself, Mr. Lamb. Are you still researching seaweed or something similar?\" [1359] \"Yes.\" [1360] \"Oh, then you know where to find it. Ocean biology, fossils, Antarctica, on the third floor. I received a new package yesterday. Although it's opened, it hasn't been sorted yet. It's in the corner over there. Take a look yourself.\" [1361] I nodded and walked sideways, making my way to the back of the store, where a dirty, unstable staircase led to the second floor. Here, there were books on Eastern history, art, medicine, and French classical literature. Every corner of this floor was draped with curtains. This was an intriguing corner unknown to the general public but open to experts and scholars. It was the exclusive storage area for books referred to as \"treasures.\" I walked over and continued up to the third floor. [1362] Here, various archaeological, zoological, and other highly regarded volumes were casually categorized. I dodged students, elderly army colonels, and priests, turning the corner of the bookshelf, stepping over many opened packages lying on the floor, filled with books, when I found that my path was blocked by a couple of lovers, lost in their own world, swaying back and forth. [1363] \"Sorry.\" I pushed them aside with force, lifted the curtain covering the door, deftly took the key from my pocket, turned the lock, and walked in. I found myself awkwardly standing in the doorway. The walls were freshly painted, and a framed print of a yak hung on them. A shiny door handle glinted on the door. I carefully tapped the door handle, and the door opened. A middle-aged woman with gray hair emerged, wearing an old-fashioned, oddly shaped pair of glasses, dressed in a black skirt, and surprisingly paired with a mint green wicker knit sweater. [1364] \"Is it you?\" She didn't greet me, getting straight to the point. \"He asked about you yesterday. He was not happy.\" She shook her head at me, as if an older family tutor shaking her head at a disappointing child. \"You could try to do better,\" she said. [1365] \"Oh, spare me, Nanny,\" I said. [1366] \"Don't call me Nanny,\" the middle-aged woman said. \"It's rude. I've told you that before.\" [1367] \"It's your fault,\" I said. \"You shouldn't talk to me like a child.\" [1368] \"You should grow up,\" she said. \"Come in and get to work.\" [1369] She pressed the bell, picked up the telephone receiver from the table, and said:\n[1370] \"Mr. Collins... Yes, I'll bring him in.\" She hung up the phone, nodding at me. [1371] I walked through the door at the back of the room and entered another room, where the air was thick with the smell of cigarettes and the smoke made it impossible to see anything. My eyes, stinging from the smoke, finally opened, and I saw the massive figure of the group leader sitting in an old, discarded armchair. Next to him was an old-fashioned desk, or rather a swivel-top writing desk. [1372] Colonel Beck took off his glasses and pushed away the revolving desk with a book open on it, looking somewhat displeased at me. [1373] \"You're finally here?\" he said. [1374] \"Yes, sir,\" I said. [1375] \"Any progress?\" [1376] \"No, sir.\" [1377] \"Well, no use, Colin. I've already told you, no use. Crescent-shaped, really!\" [1378] \"I'm still pondering,\" I began. [1379] \"Well, you're pondering. But we can't wait indefinitely for you.\" [1380] \"I admit it's based on intuition,\" I said. [1381] \"No harm in that.\" said Colonel Beck. [1382] He was a man who liked to argue. [1383] \"I've done my best cases based on intuition. But this time, your intuition seems to have failed. Have you been to the little bar yet?\" [1384] \"Yes, sir. As I told you, I've started from New Moon Street. I mean the houses on New Moon Street.\" [1385] \"I didn't think you were talking about a cake shop with French croissants, but, after all, there's no reason for me not to think so. Some places are obsessed with crescent-shaped French bread, but the bread they make doesn't taste like French bread. They freeze these breads just like everything else nowadays. That's why everything tastes bland now.\" [1386] I waited to see if he would go on about the topic. It was his hobby. Seeing the look of anticipation on my face, Colonel Beker managed to hold back. [1387] \"Have you checked it all out?\" he asked. [1388] \"Almost. But there's a little bit left.\" [1389] \"You need more time, don't you?\" [1390] \"Yes, I need more time,\" I said. \"But I don't want to go anywhere else right now. There have been some coincidences that may, just may, mean something.\" [1391] \"Cut the crap. Tell me something serious.\" [1392] \"The investigation topic, Wilbraham Crescent.\" [1393] \"Nothing came up! Any leads?\" [1394] \"I don't know.\" [1395] \"I need more concrete information, buddy.\" [1396] \"Turns out someone was murdered on Wilbraham Crescent.\" [1397] \"Who was murdered?\" [1398] \"We don't know yet. There was a business card with a name and address in his pocket, but it was fake.\" [1399] \"Hmm. Maybe a clue. The investigation hit a dead end?\" [1400] \"No, sir, but... \"\n[1401] \"I know, I know. Still... Well, what brings you here? Seeking permission to continue the investigation on Wilbraham Crescent, wherever that ridiculous place may be?\" [1402] \"It's called Crofton. Ten miles from Portbury.\" [1403] \"Yes, yes, a fine place. But what brings you here? You don't usually seek approval from your superiors. You're not one to follow orders, are you?\" [1404] \"Yes, sir, unfortunately so.\" [1405] \"Well, what is it then?\" [1406] \"I want to investigate some people.\" [1407] Colonel Baker sighed and pushed the revolving desk back into place, took a ballpoint pen out of his pocket, blew on it, and looked at me. [1408] \"Go ahead.\" [1409] \"The house is called 'Diana's Cottage.' It's actually 20 Wilbraham Crescent. It's inhabited by a Mrs. Hemm and her eighteen cats.\" [1410] \"Diana? Ah, the moon goddess! Diana's Cottage. Yes. What does Mrs. Hemm do?\" [1411] \"Nothing,\" I said. \"She just takes care of her cats.\" [1412] \"A splendid cover, I dare say,\" Colonel Beker remarked appreciatively. \"Probably so. Anything else?\" [1413] \"No,\" I said. \"There's a Mr. Lyme who lives at 62 Wilbraham Crescent. He's an engineer, supposedly, and often travels abroad.\" [1414] \"Sounds good,\" Colonel Beker said. \"Sounds very good. You want to know more about him, do you? No problem.\" [1415] \"He has a wife,\" I said. \"A very good wife, and two mischievous boys. Boys.\" [1416] \"Ah, that's nothing,\" Colonel Beck said. \"I know about that. Do you remember Pendleton? He had a wife and children. A very good wife, but the most foolish woman I've ever seen. She didn't even know that her husband wasn't a respected figure in the Oriental book trade. I remember now, he had a German wife and several daughters. He also had a wife in Switzerland. I don't know the specifics about his wives, whether it was his personal excess or just a disguise. Of course, he would say they were for cover. Well, anyway, you want to know about Mr. Rymes. Anything else?\" [1417] \"I'm not sure. There's a couple at 63. The husband is a retired professor, a Scot named MacNorton, an old man who likes to spend time gardening. There's no reason to think he or his wife are involved, but\u2014\"\n[1418] \"Okay. We'll look into it. Who are these people, by the way?\" [1419] \"Their garden adjoins the garden of the house where the murder took place, or partially adjoins.\" [1420] \"Like the French, I suppose,\" Beck said. \"My uncle's body was found in my aunt's niece's garden. What about 19?\" [1421] \"The owner is a blind woman who used to be a school teacher. She works at a blind school, and the police have thoroughly investigated her.\" [1422] \"Does she live alone?\" [1423] \"Yes.\" [1424] \"What do you think of the others?\" [1425] \"My opinion is,\" I said, \"that if any of these people did it, and I'm just saying this as a possibility, it would be relatively easy to dispose of the body in one of these houses at any time during the day, as I mentioned. That's all. There's something else I want to show you. This.\" [1426] Baker reached out to take the coin I handed him, covered in mud. [1427] \"A Czech coin? Where did you find it?\" [1428] \"In the back garden of number 19.\" [1429] \"Interesting. In the relentless pursuit of New Moon Street and the rising moon, you'll eventually find something. By the way, there's a bar nearby called 'Rising Moon'. Why don't you go there and see if you're lucky?\" [1430] \"I've already been there.\" I said. [1431] \"You're always prepared, aren't you?\" Colonel Beck said. \"Want a cigar?\" [1432] I shook my head. \"Thank you, but I don't have time today.\" [1433] \"Going back to Crofton?\" [1434] \"Yes, I have a trial to attend.\" [1435] \"It's bound to be postponed. Are you going to Crofton to chase a girl?\" [1436] \"Definitely not.\" I said alertly. [1437] Colonel Baker unexpectedly chuckled. [1438] \"Be careful, brother! The impulses of love are raising their ugly heads. How long have you known her?\" [1439] \"There's no such thing. What I mean is, there was a girl who found the body.\" [1440] \"What did she do when the body was found?\" [1441] \"She screamed.\" [1442] \"Excellent,\" said the colonel, \"she ran to you, crying on your shoulder, telling you about it. Is that right?\" [1443] \"I have no idea what you're talking about.\" I said coldly, \"Look at these.\" [1444] I handed him some photographs taken by the police. [1445] \"Who is this?\" Colonel Beck asked. [1446] \"The deceased.\" [1447] \"It sounds preposterous to me that it was the girl you were so infatuated with who killed him.\" [1448] \"You haven't heard the story yet,\" I said, \"I haven't told you.\" [1449] \"I don't need to be told,\" Colonel Beck flicked his cigar. \"Go ahead and attend your trial, my boy, watch out for that girl. Is her name Diana, or Artemis, or any name like the crescent moon?\" [1450] \"No, not at all.\" [1451] \"Oh, remember, it might be!\" [1452] * *\n[1453] [1] King of England from 1014 to 1035, who also served as King of Denmark and Norway. [1454] Chapter 14\n[1455] Narrative by Colin Lamb\n[1456] I haven't been to Whitehall House for a long time. A few years ago, it was a grand modern apartment building. Now, there are many impressive and more modern-looking buildings on both sides of the street. Going inside, I found that the building has recently been renovated. The walls have been repainted in light yellow or light green. [1457] I took the elevator upstairs and rang the doorbell at room 203. The door was opened by a polite male servant named George. He greeted me with a welcoming smile. [1458] \"Mr. Colin! It's been a while since I've seen you.\" [1459] \"Yes, you're doing well, George?\" [1460] \"I'm doing well, thank you, sir.\" [1461] I lowered my voice. \"How is he?\" [1462] George also lowered his voice, although it seemed unnecessary, as he had been speaking carefully from the beginning. [1463] \"I think, sir, that his mood is a bit low.\" [1464] I sympathetically nodded. [1465] \"This way, sir -\" He took my hat. [1466] \"Please announce me as Mr. Colin Lamb, sir.\" [1467] \"Certainly, sir.\" He opened the door and called out in a clear voice. \"Mr. Colin Lamb to see you, sir.\" [1468] He stepped back, allowing me to pass, and I entered the room. [1469] My friend, Hercule Poirot, sat in his usual large, comfortable armchair by the fireplace. I noticed a long, rectangular electric fire glowing red. It was still warm in early September, but Poirot, ever the first to sense the coming chill of autumn, had prepared accordingly. Books were neatly stacked on the floor to either side of him, and more on the desk to his left. A steaming cup of tea sat on the table to his right. I guessed it was some kind of herbal concoction. He was fond of such things and often insisted on sharing them with me. They tasted and smelled unpleasant. [1470] \"Don't get up,\" I said. But Poirot had already risen. He spread his arms and came towards me, his polished shoes gleaming. [1471] \"Ah, it is you, my friend! My young friend, Colin. But why do you call yourself Lambe? Let me think. There is a saying or proverb. It's something like 'an old sheep in sheep's clothing' \u2013 a metaphor for an elderly woman trying to dress like a young, pretty one. It doesn't suit you. Ah, I have it. You are a wolf in sheep's clothing, are you not?\" [1472] \"No, not at all,\" I said. \"It's just that using my real name in my work is not advisable, as it would involve my father to some extent. So I go by Lambe. It's short and easy to remember, and it suits me. I may be a bit self-aggrandizing, but please don't mind.\" [1473] \"Is that so?\" Poirot said. \"How is your father, my dear friend?\" [1474] \"He's doing well,\" I said. \"He's always busy with his roses or chrysanthemums or whatever flowers are in season. Time flies, and I can't remember what bloomed at that time.\" [1475] \"So he's been busy with his gardening?\" [1476] \"Everyone seems to do that as they age,\" I said. [1477] \"Not me,\" said Hercule Poirot. \"I used to love growing zucchinis, yes, but that's all in the past. If you want the best flowers, why not go to the florist? I think that good detective is going to write his memoirs?\" [1478] \"He has started,\" I said. \"But he found that there was a lot that needed to be deleted, and in the end, he concluded that what remained was too dull to be worth writing down.\" [1479] \"It's a habit that comes with the job, yes,\" said Poirot. \"It's unfortunate. Because your father has some very interesting stories. I admire him. You know, his methods are very interesting to me. He's always so straightforward. He uses methods that no one has ever used before.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."